# Abbreviation

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

An abbreviation is a short form of a longer term, such as “EV” for “electric vehicle”. The HTML `abbr` element marks it and can carry the full term in its `title` attribute.

Civica’s rule is to write the full term first, with the abbreviation in brackets, then use the short form. People should never need to hover to understand a card. This page owns the `abbr` element. The [A to Z](../content/style-guides/a-to-z.html#abbreviations-acronyms-and-initialisms) owns the wording rule.

This page covers:

- Abbreviation
  - `<abbr>`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

`abbr` is an inline element that holds the short form as its text. Its only special attribute is `title`, which should hold the full expansion. Browsers usually draw a dotted underline under `abbr[title]`.

`abbr` is not a Home Assistant element and has no theme variables of its own. It inherits the text colour from `primary-text-color` and the size from the surrounding text.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-abbr"><code>&lt;abbr&gt;</code></h3>

- With `title`: the short form is underlined with dots and shows the expansion as a tooltip on hover.
- Without `title`: the element has no visible or spoken effect. Do not use it as decoration.

A tooltip does not reach everyone. Touch users have no hover, keyboard users cannot focus the tooltip, and screen readers do not reliably read it. So use `abbr` as an extra, never as the only place the expansion appears.

Do not depend on the element in a Home Assistant markdown card. Write the expansion into the text instead.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- It marks the short form in a way that assistive technology can use.
- It costs nothing and needs no integration.
- The expansion in `title` is invisible on touch screens and to keyboard users.
- The tooltip disappears when the pointer moves, so people cannot read it at their own pace.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use the full term first, then the abbreviation in brackets: “Electric vehicle (EV) charger”. After that, use “EV” alone on the same page. Do not explain common abbreviations such as USB, LED or TV.

Do not use an abbreviation in a title, button label or state if a short plain word would do. For a term used across many cards, link the first use to a glossary entry instead.

Success criteria that apply:

- 3.1.4 Abbreviations (AAA): give a way to find the expansion.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard and 1.4.13 Content on Hover or Focus: a `title` tooltip fails both if it is the only route.
- 3.1.3 Unusual Words (AAA): explain terms people may not know.

Undefined abbreviations raise the effort of reading, especially for people with memory or language difficulties. Expanding on first use removes that effort.

